pg_bp_arc -- Design and Implementation Notes
=============================================

This document is intended for engineers maintaining or extending the
pg_bp_arc code.  It covers the ARC algorithm theory, the PostgreSQL
implementation details, deviations from the textbook algorithm, and
known limitations.

Source file: contrib/pg_bp_arc/pg_bp_arc.c


A. Algorithm Theory
===================

ARC (Adaptive Replacement Cache) maintains four logical lists and one
adaptive parameter.  The cache holds c pages total (c = nbuffers).

Lists
-----

    T1   Recently accessed pages.  A page enters T1 the first time it
         is loaded into the cache.  Pages on T1 have been accessed
         exactly once since being brought into cache.

    T2   Frequently accessed pages.  When a page on T1 is accessed a
         second time, it is promoted to T2.  Subsequent hits on T2
         move the page to the MRU end of T2.

    B1   Ghost list for T1.  When a page is evicted from T1, its
         identity (tag) is kept in B1 as a ghost entry.  The ghost
         entry has no associated buffer -- it only records that the
         page was recently in T1.

    B2   Ghost list for T2.  Same as B1, but for pages evicted from
         T2.

Ghost entries allow ARC to detect that an evicted page has been
re-requested, which drives the adaptation logic.

Invariants:

    |T1| + |T2| <= c           (resident pages fit in cache)
    |T1| + |B1| <= c           (T1 directory bound)
    |T1| + |T2| + |B1| + |B2| <= 2c  (total directory bound)

Adaptive parameter: target_T1_size
-----------------------------------

target_T1_size (called "p" in the original paper) controls the desired
split between T1 and T2.  It starts at c/2 and adapts over time:

    B1 hit (page found in B1 ghost list):
        T1 was too small.  The page was evicted from T1 recently and
        is being accessed again.  Increase target_T1_size.

        delta = max(|B2| / |B1|, 1)
        target_T1_size = min(target_T1_size + delta, c)

    B2 hit (page found in B2 ghost list):
        T2 was too small.  The page was evicted from T2 recently and
        is being accessed again.  Decrease target_T1_size.

        delta = max(|B1| / |B2|, 1)
        target_T1_size = max(target_T1_size - delta, 0)

The delta formula is asymmetric: the ratio of the opposite ghost list
to the hit ghost list scales the step size.  When one ghost list is
much larger than the other, the step is small (the signal is weak);
when the lists are similar in size, the step is larger.

Eviction decision
-----------------

When a new page must be loaded and the cache is full:

    If |T1| >= max(1, target_T1_size):
        Evict the LRU page from T1 (move it to B1 as a ghost).
    Else:
        Evict the LRU page from T2 (move it to B2 as a ghost).

This means that T1 grows up to target_T1_size before evictions shift
to T2.

Operation costs:

    Hit:      O(1) -- spinlock acquire, doubly-linked list remove + insert
    Miss:     O(1) -- ghost hash lookup, list insert
    Eviction: O(1) amortized -- pop from LRU of T1 or T2


B. Implementation Details
=========================

Cache Directory Blocks (CDBs)
-----------------------------

All list nodes are stored in a flat array of ArcCDB structures.  The
total number of CDB entries is 2 * nbuffers (ncdb).  This gives
enough entries for every buffer to be on T1 or T2, with an equal
number available for ghost entries on B1 and B2.

Each ArcCDB contains:

    prev         int     Previous node in doubly-linked list (-1 = head)
    next         int     Next node in doubly-linked list (-1 = tail)
    list         int     Which ARC list (T1, T2, B1, B2, or UNUSED)
    buf_tag      BufferTag   Page identity (relfilenode, fork, block)
    buf_id       int     Pool-local buffer index (-1 for ghost entries)
    ghost_next   int     Next in ghost hash chain (-1 = end of chain)

The doubly-linked lists use integer indices into the CDB array rather
than pointers, since the entire structure lives in DSM (dynamic shared
memory) and must be position-independent.

Each ARC list has a head and tail index stored in ArcControl:

    list_head[4]    Head (LRU end) of each list
    list_tail[4]    Tail (MRU end) of each list
    list_size[4]    Current count of entries on each list

Free CDB list
--------------

Unused CDB entries are linked into a singly-linked free list using
the .next field.  ctl->free_cdb_list points to the first free entry.

When the free list is empty, a ghost entry is recycled:

    If |T1| + |B1| >= c:
        Recycle the LRU entry from B1 (prefer B1).
    Else if |B2| > 0:
        Recycle the LRU entry from B2.
    Else if |B1| > 0:
        Recycle the LRU entry from B1 (fallback).

Recycling removes the ghost from its hash chain and list, then
returns the CDB for reuse.

Ghost hash table
----------------

Ghost entries (B1 and B2) must be looked up by BufferTag during the
on_miss callback.  A separate hash table provides O(1) expected-time
lookup.

    Size:       Power-of-2, >= ncdb (computed as pg_nextpower2_32(ncdb))
    Bucket:     hash(buf_tag) & (ghost_hash_size - 1)
    Chaining:   Through the ghost_next field in ArcCDB (integer index)
    Stored in:  int array immediately after the CDB array in DSM

Only ghost entries (B1/B2) are inserted into the ghost hash table.
Resident entries (T1/T2) are looked up via buf_to_cdb[] instead.

buf_to_cdb[] mapping
--------------------

An integer array of size nbuffers maps pool-local buffer IDs to CDB
indices:

    buf_to_cdb[pool_local_id] = cdb_index   (or -1 if untracked)

This is used by on_hit and on_evict to find the CDB for a buffer in
O(1) time without scanning any list.

Memory layout
-------------

All ARC state for a pool is allocated as a single contiguous block
in dynamic shared memory (DSM).  The layout is:

    Offset 0:                   ArcControl header (fixed size)
    After ArcControl:           ArcCDB[ncdb]      (2 * nbuffers entries)
    After CDB array:            int ghost_hash[ghost_hash_size]
    After ghost hash:           int buf_to_cdb[nbuffers]

Accessor macros compute pointers into this block:

    ARC_CDB(ctl)          -> ArcCDB array
    ARC_GHOST_HASH(ctl)   -> ghost hash bucket array
    ARC_BUF_TO_CDB(ctl)   -> buffer-to-CDB index array

The total shared memory size is computed by ArcShmemSize() and
allocated by the buffer pool infrastructure before ArcShmemInit()
is called.

Per-backend state: ArcBackendState
-----------------------------------

The on_miss and get_victim callbacks are called as separate steps
within a single BufferAlloc invocation.  Information must be passed
between them:

    ghost_cdb     CDB index of the ghost hit found during on_miss,
                  or -1 for a complete miss.  Used by on_new_tag to
                  decide whether to promote a ghost entry or allocate
                  a fresh CDB.

    evict_from    Which list (T1 or T2) get_victim should try first,
                  based on |T1| vs. target_T1_size at on_miss time.

    vacuum_hint   True if VACUUM is active for this backend.  Set by
                  hint_vacuum callback.

Each backend maintains a small array of ArcBackendState (one per pool
it has accessed), keyed by the ArcControl pointer.  This prevents
cross-pool state leakage when multiple ARC pools exist.

Concurrency: single spinlock
-----------------------------

ctl->arc_lock is a PostgreSQL slock_t (spinlock) that protects all
mutable fields in ArcControl, the CDB array, the ghost hash table,
and the buf_to_cdb mapping.

Every on_hit, on_miss, on_evict, and on_new_tag call acquires this
lock.  The critical sections are short (a few pointer updates), but
the lock is acquired on every buffer access.

Statistics counters (pg_atomic_uint64) can be read without the lock,
but writes go through PoolStatIncrement batching (see below).


C. Modifications from Textbook ARC
===================================

VACUUM integration
------------------

In the textbook algorithm, every new page enters T1 at the MRU end.
In this implementation, when VACUUM is active (vacuum_hint is true
or the on_new_tag vacuum_hint parameter is set), new pages are
inserted at the LRU end of T1 instead.

This means VACUUM-loaded pages sit at the eviction end of T1 and are
the first to be evicted.  Without this, a VACUUM scanning a large
table would push the entire working set out of cache.

The relevant code is in ArcOnNewTag:

    if (vacuum_hint || state->vacuum_hint)
        arc_lru_insert(ctl, cdb_arr, cdb_idx, ARC_LIST_T1);
    else
        arc_mru_insert(ctl, cdb_arr, cdb_idx, ARC_LIST_T1);

Ghost CDB validation
--------------------

The ghost_cdb value set during on_miss can become stale if an
intervening operation (such as ExtendBufferedRelShared for FSM or
VM pages) bypasses the normal on_miss/get_victim/on_new_tag sequence.

ArcOnNewTag validates the ghost CDB before using it:

    if (cdb_idx >= ctl->ncdb ||
        (cdb_arr[cdb_idx].list != ARC_LIST_B1 &&
         cdb_arr[cdb_idx].list != ARC_LIST_B2))
    {
        /* Stale ghost_cdb -- treat as a complete miss */
        goto complete_miss;
    }

This prevents a stale ghost_cdb from corrupting the list structure.

Free buffer scan (cold start optimization)
-------------------------------------------

During cold start or pool warm-up, not all buffers have been loaded
yet.  The textbook algorithm always evicts from T1 or T2, but that
is wasteful when free buffers exist.

ArcGetVictim checks whether T1 + T2 < nbuffers.  If so, it scans
buf_to_cdb[] for a buffer with index -1 (untracked) and tries to
pin it.  Only when no free buffers remain does it fall back to
evicting from T1 or T2.

This avoids the need for a separate free buffer list in shared
memory.  The scan is O(n) in the worst case but only runs during
warm-up when most entries are free.

Trickle writer integration
--------------------------

ARC implements the trickle_iter_begin/next/end callbacks for the
per-pool trickle writer (background writer).  The iterator walks:

    Phase 0: T1 from LRU to MRU
    Phase 1: T2 from LRU to MRU

For each CDB with a resident buffer (buf_id >= 0), it checks whether
the buffer is dirty, valid, and unpinned.  If so, it yields the
global buffer ID for the trickle writer to flush.

T1 is scanned first because T1-LRU pages are the most likely
eviction candidates.  Writing them proactively reduces the chance
that get_victim must flush a dirty buffer synchronously.

Stat counter batching
---------------------

Statistics are not updated by direct atomic increment on every
operation.  Instead, PoolStatIncrement is used, which maintains
per-backend local counters and flushes them to the shared
pg_atomic_uint64 counters every 256 increments.

    PoolStatIncrement(&arc_local_stats[slot], &ctl->stat_xxx);

The local counters are declared as:

    static uint64 arc_local_stats[ARC_NUM_STATS];

This reduces atomic operation overhead on the stats counters from
once per buffer access to once per 256 accesses.  The shared counters
can still be read at any time (they may lag by up to 256 per backend).


D. Concurrency and Scalability
==============================

The single spinlock (arc_lock) is the primary scalability limitation.

Every cache hit requires:

    1. SpinLockAcquire(&ctl->arc_lock)
    2. Remove CDB from current list position (4 pointer updates)
    3. Insert CDB at MRU of T2 (4 pointer updates)
    4. SpinLockRelease(&ctl->arc_lock)

On a system with N cores all doing buffer lookups, this serializes
hit processing.  The critical section is small (no memory allocation,
no I/O, just integer assignments), but at very high access rates the
spinlock becomes a bottleneck.

For comparison, CAR (pg_bp_car) uses a single atomic reference bit
set on hit, with no lock.  The lock is only needed during clock sweep
(eviction), which happens much less frequently than hits.

Stat counters use pg_atomic_uint64 and can be read from pg_stat_arc
without acquiring the spinlock.  However, writes use the batched
PoolStatIncrement mechanism to avoid one atomic increment per access.

The spinlock is NOT held during:

  - I/O operations (buffer reads/writes)
  - Buffer header pin/unpin CAS operations in get_victim
  - Trickle writer I/O (only the iterator snapshot is taken under lock)

The spinlock IS held during:

  - on_hit: list move (T1->T2 or T2 MRU reposition)
  - on_miss: ghost lookup + target_T1_size update
  - on_evict: T1/T2 -> B1/B2 move + ghost hash insert
  - on_new_tag: ghost promote or fresh CDB allocation + list insert

Each of these critical sections is O(1) in the number of list
operations performed.


E. Memory Overhead
==================

Per buffer, ARC allocates:

    2 ArcCDB entries           2 * sizeof(ArcCDB)
    1 ghost hash bucket        sizeof(int)          (amortized)
    1 buf_to_cdb entry         sizeof(int)

ArcCDB structure size (assuming 64-bit system, typical padding):

    prev           4 bytes (int)
    next           4 bytes (int)
    list           4 bytes (int)
    buf_tag        ~20 bytes (BufferTag: RelFileLocator + ForkNumber + BlockNumber)
    buf_id         4 bytes (int)
    ghost_next     4 bytes (int)
    padding        ~8 bytes (alignment)
    -----------------------------------
    Total:         ~48 bytes per CDB

Per buffer overhead breakdown:

    2 CDB entries:           ~96 bytes
    ghost hash (amortized):  ~8 bytes (power-of-2 sized, ~2x ncdb ints)
    buf_to_cdb:              4 bytes
    -----------------------------------
    Total per buffer:        ~100-120 bytes

For a pool with 131072 buffers (1 GB at 8 KB pages), the ARC metadata
consumes roughly 13-16 MB.

The ArcControl header itself is fixed-size (a few hundred bytes for
the spinlock, list head/tail arrays, and atomic stat counters).


F. Performance Characteristics
==============================

    Operation      Cost               Notes
    ---------      ----               -----
    Hit (T1/T2)    O(1)               Spinlock + 2 list ops (remove + insert)
    Miss           O(1)               Ghost hash lookup + list insert
    Eviction       O(1) amortized     Pop LRU of T1 or T2
    Ghost recycle  O(1)               Pop LRU of B1 or B2, remove from hash
    Free buf scan  O(n) worst case    Only during cold start / warm-up
    Trickle iter   O(n) per pass      Walks T1 then T2 once

Worst case for get_victim: all buffers in the pool are pinned.  The
code walks the entire primary list (T1 or T2), then the entire
secondary list, checking each buffer's pin count.  If no unpinned
buffer is found, it raises an ERROR.  This is the same behavior as
the standard clock sweep when all buffers are pinned.

Cache performance relative to LRU:

  - Scan-resistant: A single sequential scan of a table larger than
    the cache does not evict the entire working set.  The scan pages
    enter T1 and are evicted from T1 without disturbing T2.

  - Frequency-aware: Pages accessed multiple times are promoted to
    T2, which is protected from eviction as long as target_T1_size
    has not shrunk to zero.

  - Self-tuning: The target_T1_size parameter adjusts automatically.
    No DBA intervention is needed to balance recency vs. frequency.


