H. Known Limitations and Future Work
=====================================

Spinlock contention on many-core systems
    The single arc_lock serializes all hit processing.  On systems
    with 64+ cores running a heavily concurrent OLTP workload, this
    can become the dominant bottleneck.  Possible mitigations include
    partitioning the ARC lists (one lock per partition) or switching
    to a lock-free structure.  CAR's approach of using atomic reference
    bits eliminates the per-hit lock entirely.

No prefetch_hint callback
    The arc_pool_routine sets prefetch_hint = NULL.  CAR implements
    this callback to check ghost lists on prefetch and pre-adapt the
    target parameter.  Adding prefetch_hint to ARC would require
    acquiring the spinlock for ghost lookup, which may not be
    worthwhile given the existing per-hit lock cost.

No VACUUM-specific eviction acceleration
    VACUUM pages are inserted at the LRU end of T1, making them the
    first eviction candidates.  However, they still occupy T1 slots
    until normal eviction removes them.  A dedicated VACUUM eviction
    path that immediately frees the buffer without placing it on T1
    could reduce T1 pollution during large VACUUM operations.  The
    current approach is simple and works well in practice for typical
    VACUUM workloads.

Cold start free buffer scan
    The O(n) scan of buf_to_cdb[] during warm-up could be replaced
    with a dedicated free buffer list if startup performance becomes
    important.  In practice the scan only runs when many buffers are
    free, and each scanned buffer is either immediately usable or
    quickly skipped.

Debug validation overhead
    ArcValidateLists (enabled in USE_ASSERT_CHECKING builds) walks
    all four lists and the buf_to_cdb array on every on_evict and
    on_new_tag call.  This is intentionally expensive and should
    never be enabled in production builds.
